<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ohthere of Hålogaland</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. c. 890</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Ottar</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ohthere of Hålogaland was a Norwegian chieftain and trader who visited the court of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-the-great">Alfred of Wessex</a> around 890. His account, added to an Old English version of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orosius">Orosius</a>, describes his life in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, his voyage round the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-cape">North Cape</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-sea">White Sea</a>, the tribute he took from the Sámi and his journey south to the market of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaupang">Kaupang</a> and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hedeby">Hedeby</a>. It is the earliest written description of Norway.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ohthere said that he lived furthest north of all Norwegians, in Halgoland, identified today as Hålogaland; suggested places for his home include Senja, Kvaløya and the land around the Malangen fjord, all near Tromsø. He called himself a leading man of his country and wealthy, with 600 tame reindeer, though he had no more than twenty <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, twenty sheep and twenty pigs. His main wealth was the tax paid by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sami-people">Sami</a>, of whom the highest-born gave 15 marten skins, 5 reindeer skins, a bear skin, feathers, a coat of bear or otter skin and two ship's ropes of whale or seal skin. He also hunted walrus and whales, and brought walrus tusks to King Alfred.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Sailing north to learn how far the land reached, he followed the coast for six days, turned east round the land near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-cape">North Cape</a>, then south, and after some fifteen days in all entered a large river beyond which lived the Beormas, whose language he said was almost that of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sami-people">Sami</a>. Historians place their land, Bjarmaland, near Kandalaksha or on the Northern Dvina. He did not cross the river for fear of unfrið, usually translated as hostility.</p><p>His southern route ran along the length of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> to a port called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaupang">Sciringes heal</a>, usually identified with Skiringssal in Vestfold, and on to the Danish trading settlement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hedeby">Hedeby</a> at the head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schlei">Schlei</a> inlet. Here the earliest copy of the Old English Orosius gives the first known mention of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, as dena mearc, and his norðweg comes between 40 and 80 years before the runic Nuruiak on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jelling">Jelling</a> stones. Why he came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Wessex</a> is not recorded; the anthropologist Ian Whitaker found no evidence for the theories linking his visit to Alfred's navy, to flight from King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harald-fairhair">Harald Fairhair</a> or to a lost fortune.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Nothing is known of Ohthere beyond his account. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-wadsworth-longfellow">Henry Wadsworth Longfellow</a> dramatised his audience with Alfred in the poem &quot;The Discoverer of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-cape">North Cape</a>&quot;, and he appears in Gwendolyn Bowers's novel The Lost Dragon of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Wessex</a> of 1957 and in Vladimir Nabokov's Pale Fire.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ohthere of Hålogaland” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Around 890 <b>Ohthere</b>, a Hålogaland chieftain, described his life and his voyage round the North Cape to King Alfred of Wessex, the earliest written account of the region.</blockquote><a href="/state/halogaland">Hålogaland</a></li><li><blockquote>The kings controlled Kaupang, the trading town the traveller <b>Ohthere</b> called Sciringes heal, where goods from the Frankish, Danish and Arab worlds were exchanged.</blockquote><a href="/state/vestfold">Vestfold</a></li></ul></section>
